  "query_preview": "..\n\nLow-level anticyclone and surface ridge depart this evening ahead of \na shortwave descending on the Great Lakes region tonight into \nWednesday. Temperatures should be bit a warmer overnight due to \nthickening cloud cover ahead of the wave, with lows in the upper 50s \nto around 60F.\n\nExpect a dry start on Wednesday with strengthening gradient flow as \nwinds back south-southwesterly. Pre-frontal/warm sector moistening \nwill be ongoing, well ahead of the boundary, marked by a sharp \nincrease in 925-700 mb ThetaE advection. The parent shortwave should \nslow upon arrival, once the vertically stacked low reaches Lake \nSuperior by 12Z Wednesday. This slowing trend is supported in the \n12Z model data leading to increasing confidence in a later start \ntime for rainfall across most of Southeast Michigan. With the \nexception of the Tri-Cities, the rest of the forecast area should \nstay rain-free prior to 15Z, if not 18Z. Showers and thunderstorms \nshould fire along the frontal slope once weak 800 mb (5 kft) capping \nerodes during the diurnal ramp-up. Non-convective gusts will mix \ndown, occasionally reaching 25-30 mph.\n\nA low-end severe risk exists Wednesday afternoon and evening as \nMLCAPE builds above 1000 J/kg, although 0-6 km shear only improves \nto 25-30 knots. SRH also isn't particularly impressive and struggles \nto break 100 m2/s2, but the longer that winds stay backed/southerly, \nthe risk would increase for a low-end weak/short-lived tornado \nthreat. The main concern should be for gusty/damaging winds. Latest \nSPC SWODY2 (Marginal with Slight south of I-96) appears to align \nwell with a subset of latest ML data. Storm coverage should \ngenerally be broken/linear, positioned along the front, and ought to \ndepart/conclude once the boundary exits by about 03Z. A weak \nsecondary surface low may develop over The Thumb, and would offer \nrain potential into the overnight hours.\n\nThe shortwave will flatten and slowly get absorbed downstream into \nthe larger-scale trough feature. Weak CVA could persist through \nThursday, providing enough ascent atop shallow boundary-layer \nsaturation to spur a few light showers/sprinkles, mainly during the \nevening hours. Surface high pressure builds in from the UP Friday \nwith modest northerly flow helping limit highs to the upper 70s as \n850 mb temperatures drop into the single digits (Celsius). The \nsurface ridge exits into the eastern Great Lakes over the weekend \nwith return flow shifting gradient winds southwesterly in advance of \nthe next possible system.",
